obj.std-algebra.clifford-algebra.formal:
A \emph{Clifford algebra} of the real quadratic space $(V, Q)$ is a pair $(\Cl(V, Q), \iota)$ of an associative unital real algebra and a linear map $\iota : V \to \Cl(V, Q)$ with
\begin{equation}
\iota(v)^{2} = Q(v)\, 1 \qquad \text{for all } v \in V,
\end{equation}
which is universal: for every associative unital real algebra $A$ and linear map $f : V \to A$ with $f(v)^{2} = Q(v)\,1$, there is exactly one unital algebra homomorphism $\bar f : \Cl(V, Q) \to A$ with $\bar f \circ \iota = f$. We identify $v$ with $\iota(v)$ (justified by \claimref{std-algebra.blade-basis}) and call its product the \emph{geometric product}. $\Cl(p,q,r) = \Cl(\R^{p,q,r})$ and $\Cl(p,q) = \Cl(p,q,0)$.

obj.std-algebra.clifford-group.formal:
The \emph{twisted adjoint action} of an invertible $g \in \Cl(V, Q)$ is $\Ad_g(x) = \hat{g}\, x\, g^{-1}$. The \emph{Clifford group} is
\begin{equation}
\Gamma(V, Q) = \{ g \in \Cl(V, Q) \text{ invertible} : \Ad_g(V) = V \}.
\end{equation}
A \emph{versor} is a product $u_1 \cdots u_k$ of invertible vectors (\claimref{std-algebra.inverse-of-vector}); it is even or odd with $k$. Versors form a subgroup of $\Gamma(V, Q)$, and for a single vector $\Ad_u = s_u$ is the reflection of \claimref{std-algebra.reflection}.

obj.std-algebra.hyperbolic-plane.formal:
A \emph{hyperbolic plane} is a nondegenerate real quadratic space $H$ of dimension $2$ that contains a nonzero null vector. A \emph{hyperbolic pair} in a quadratic space is a pair $(u, w)$ of null vectors with $B(u, w) = \tfrac12$, equivalently
\begin{equation}
u w + w u = 1
\end{equation}
in the Clifford algebra. By \claimref{std-algebra.witt-decomposition}, every hyperbolic plane is spanned by a hyperbolic pair, and every hyperbolic plane is isometric to $\R^{1,1}$: with $e = u + w$ and $f = u - w$ one has $Q(e) = 1$, $Q(f) = -1$ and $B(e, f) = 0$.

obj.std-algebra.left-contraction.formal:
The \emph{left contraction} is the bilinear product defined on homogeneous elements by
\begin{equation}
A_r \rfloor B_s = \begin{cases} \langle A_r B_s \rangle_{s-r} & s \ge r, \\ 0 & s < r, \end{cases}
\end{equation}
extended bilinearly. It is the store's inner product. For vectors, $a \rfloor b = B(a, b)$; for a scalar, $\lambda \rfloor B = \lambda B$. (Doran and Lasenby use the symmetric inner product $\langle A_r B_s \rangle_{|r-s|}$ with scalars excluded; the two agree when $1 \le r \le s$.)

obj.std-algebra.outer-product.formal:
The \emph{outer product} on $\Cl(V, Q)$ is the bilinear product defined on homogeneous elements by
\begin{equation}
A_r \wedge B_s = \langle A_r B_s \rangle_{r+s}, \qquad A_r \in \Cl^{r},\ B_s \in \Cl^{s},
\end{equation}
and extended bilinearly to all multivectors. Scalars act by ordinary multiplication: $\lambda \wedge B = \lambda B$.

obj.std-algebra.pin-spin.formal:
Let $(V, Q)$ be a real quadratic space. The groups
\begin{equation}
\Pin(V, Q) = \{ u_1 \cdots u_k : Q(u_i) = \pm 1 \}, \qquad \Spin(V, Q) = \Pin(V, Q) \cap \Cl^{+}, \qquad \Spin^{+}(V, Q) = \{ R \in \Spin(V, Q) : R \tilde{R} = 1 \}
\end{equation}
are the versors of unit vectors, the even ones, and the even ones of reverse-norm $+1$. For a versor of unit vectors $g \tilde{g} = \prod_i Q(u_i) = \pm 1$ (\claimref{std-algebra.inverse-of-vector}). We write $\Pin(p,q)$, $\Spin(p,q)$, $\Spin^{+}(p,q)$ for $\R^{p,q}$; $\mathrm{O}(p,q)$, $\mathrm{SO}(p,q)$ are the isometries and those of determinant $1$, and $\mathrm{SO}^{+}(p,q)$ is the subgroup of $\mathrm{SO}(p,q)$ of products of reflections $s_{u_1} \cdots s_{u_{2m}}$ with $\prod Q(u_i) > 0$ (the kernel of the spinor norm). In the store $\mathrm{SO}^{+}$ always means this kernel. For nondegenerate $V$ it is the identity component of $\mathrm{SO}(V, Q)$, a standard fact cited from Porteous and from Lounesto, Ch. 17, and not proved here.

obj.std-algebra.quadratic-space.formal:
A \emph{real quadratic space} $(V, Q)$ is a finite-dimensional real vector space $V$ with a map $Q : V \to \R$ such that $Q(\lambda v) = \lambda^{2} Q(v)$ for all $\lambda \in \R$, $v \in V$, and
\begin{equation}
B(u, v) = \tfrac12\bigl(Q(u + v) - Q(u) - Q(v)\bigr)
\end{equation}
is bilinear. $B$ is the \emph{polar form} of $Q$. The form is not assumed nondegenerate. Vectors $u, v$ are \emph{orthogonal} when $B(u, v) = 0$; a vector $v$ is \emph{null} when $Q(v) = 0$. An \emph{isometry} $f : (V, Q) \to (W, Q')$ is a linear map with $Q'(f v) = Q(v)$ for all $v$.

obj.std-algebra.radical.formal:
The \emph{radical} of a real quadratic space $(V, Q)$ with polar form $B$ is
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{rad} V = \{ a \in V : B(a, x) = 0 \ \text{for all } x \in V \}.
\end{equation}
$(V, Q)$ is \emph{nondegenerate} when $\operatorname{rad} V = 0$, and \emph{degenerate} otherwise. Every vector of the radical is null.

primer.commutation-rule.formal:
If the element $x$ lies in the subalgebra generated by vectors orthogonal to a vector $v$, then
\begin{equation}
v\,x = \hat{x}\,v, \qquad x\,v = v\,\hat{x} .
\end{equation}

primer.twisted-adjoint-action.formal:
Let $g = u_1 \cdots u_k$ be a versor and define, on the whole algebra,
\begin{equation}
\tau_g(x) \;=\;
\begin{cases}
\,g\, x\, g^{-1} & g \text{ even},\\[2pt]
\,g\, \hat{x}\, g^{-1} & g \text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
Then each $\tau_g$ is an algebra automorphism, and on vectors it restricts to the twisted adjoint $\Ad_g(x) = \hat{g} x g^{-1}$. For a single invertible vector $u$,
\[
\Ad_u(x) \;=\; -\,u x u^{-1} \;=\; x - 2\,\frac{x \cdot u}{Q(u)}\,u ,
\]
the reflection in the hyperplane orthogonal to $u$; consequently $g \mapsto \Ad_g$ maps $\Pin(p,q)$ onto $O(p,q)$ with kernel $\{\pm 1\}$.

std-algebra.bivector-exponential.formal:
Let $A \in \Cl^{2}(V, Q)$ with $A^{2} = \lambda \in \R$ (true for every simple bivector $A = a \wedge b$, and for every bivector when $n \le 3$). Then $A^{2k} = \lambda^{k}$, $A^{2k+1} = \lambda^{k} A$, and $e^{A} = \sum_k A^{k}/k!$ is
\begin{equation}
e^{A} =
\begin{cases}
\cos\theta + \dfrac{\sin\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = -\theta^{2} < 0,\\[6pt]
\cosh\theta + \dfrac{\sinh\theta}{\theta} A, & \lambda = \theta^{2} > 0,\\[6pt]
1 + A, & \lambda = 0 .
\end{cases}
\end{equation}
In each case $e^{A} (e^{A})^{\sim} = e^{A} e^{-A} = 1$. For orthogonal $a, b$ with $Q(a) \ne 0$, $e^{\phi\, \hat{a}\hat{b}}$ (unit $\hat{a}, \hat{b}$, or $\hat{b}$ null) is the product of two invertible vectors, hence a rotor (\claimref{obj.std-algebra.rotor}): a rotation when $(a \wedge b)^{2} < 0$, a boost when $> 0$, and a null rotation or translation when $= 0$.

std-algebra.bivector-lie-algebra.formal:
For $A, C \in \Cl^{2}(V, Q)$ and $x \in V$, with the commutator product $A \times X = \tfrac12(AX - XA)$:
\begin{equation}
A \times C = \langle A C \rangle_2 \in \Cl^{2}, \qquad A \times x = \langle A x \rangle_1 \in V, \qquad B(A \times x, y) + B(x, A \times y) = 0 .
\end{equation}
So $\Cl^{2}$ is a Lie algebra under $\times$, and $A \mapsto (x \mapsto A \times x)$ is a Lie algebra homomorphism $\Cl^{2} \to \so(V, Q)$, the skew-adjoint maps of $(V, Q)$. When $(V, Q)$ is nondegenerate it is an isomorphism, so $\dim \so(V, Q) = \binom{n}{2}$. The scalar part is cyclic: $\langle X Y \rangle_0 = \langle Y X \rangle_0$.

std-algebra.chevalley-pairing.formal:
In the Fock model of \claimref{std-algebra.fock-representation}, let $\tau$ be the reversal of $\Lambda W$ ($\tau(x_1 \wedge \dots \wedge x_m) = x_m \wedge \dots \wedge x_1$) and $\operatorname{top}(z)$ the coefficient of $w_1 \wedge \dots \wedge w_k$ in $z$. Set $\beta(x, y) = \operatorname{top}(\tau(x) \wedge y)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\beta$ is nondegenerate and pairs $\Lambda^{m} W$ only with $\Lambda^{k-m} W$; so it pairs each parity with itself for $k$ even, and even with odd for $k$ odd;
\item[(ii)] $\beta(y, x) = (-1)^{k(k-1)/2}\, \beta(x, y)$;
\item[(iii)] every vector is self-adjoint: $\beta(u x, y) = \beta(x, u y)$ for $u \in V$; hence $\beta(a x, y) = \beta(x, \tilde{a} y)$ for all $a \in \Cl(V, Q)$, and $\beta(R x, R y) = \beta(x, y)$ whenever $\tilde{R} R = 1$.
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.cl11-matrix.formal:
The Clifford algebra of any hyperbolic plane $H$ is isomorphic to $\Mat_2(\R)$: if $(u, w)$ is a hyperbolic pair spanning $H$, the assignment
\begin{equation}
u \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix}, \qquad w \mapsto \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
\end{equation}
extends to an isomorphism $\Cl(H) \to \Mat_2(\R)$, under which $uw$ and $wu$ are the complementary idempotents $\operatorname{diag}(1, 0)$ and $\operatorname{diag}(0, 1)$. In particular $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$. Also $\Cl(2,0) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$, by $e_1 \mapsto \operatorname{diag}(1, -1)$, $e_2 \mapsto \begin{psmallmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 1 & 0 \end{psmallmatrix}$.

std-algebra.complex-clifford-algebra.formal:
A \emph{complex quadratic space} is a complex vector space $V$ of dimension $n$ with a quadratic form $Q : V \to \C$ whose polar form $B$ is $\C$-bilinear. Its Clifford algebra $\Cl(V, Q)$ is defined by the universal property of \claimref{obj.std-algebra.clifford-algebra} with ``real'' replaced by ``complex'' throughout (complex unital algebras, $\C$-linear maps).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\Cl(V, Q)$ exists and is unique up to unique isomorphism; for any basis of $V$ the $2^{n}$ increasing products form a $\C$-basis, degenerate forms included; the grade involution, reversion and the $\Z_2$-grading are as in the real case. For a real quadratic space $(V_\R, Q_\R)$, $\Cl(V_\R, Q_\R) \otimes_\R \C \cong \Cl(V_\R \otimes \C, Q_\C)$.
\item[(ii)] If $Q$ is nondegenerate, $V$ has a basis with $Q(e_i) = 1$ and $B(e_i, e_j) = 0$ for $i \ne j$. So $(V, Q) \cong (\C^{n}, \sum_i z_i^{2})$ and $\Cl(V, Q) \cong \Cl_n(\C)$ depends only on $n$.
\item[(iii)] Let $Q$ be nondegenerate and $n = 2k$. Every totally null subspace has dimension at most $k$. For every totally null $W$ of dimension $k$ (for instance $w_j = \tfrac12(e_{2j-1} + \mathrm{i}\, e_{2j})$) there is a totally null $W'$ with bases $w_j \in W$, $v_j \in W'$ such that $B(v_i, w_j) = \tfrac12 \delta_{ij}$, and $V = W \oplus W'$. Letting $w \in W$ act on $\Lambda W$ by $w \wedge{}$ and $v \in W'$ by the contraction $\iota_{2B(v, \cdot)}$ gives an isomorphism $\Cl(V, Q) \cong \End_\C(\Lambda W) \cong \Mat_{2^{k}}(\C)$; $f = v_1 w_1 \cdots v_k w_k$ is a primitive idempotent and $\Cl(V, Q) f \cong \Lambda W$ is a minimal left ideal with basis $w_I f$. Vectors act oddly, and $\Cl^{+}(V, Q) \cong \End(\Lambda^{\mathrm{even}} W) \oplus \End(\Lambda^{\mathrm{odd}} W)$: the two half-spinor modules, each of dimension $2^{k-1}$.
\item[(iv)] Let $Q$ be nondegenerate and $n = 2k + 1$. Then $\omega_\C = \mathrm{i}^{\,n(n-1)/2} e_1 \cdots e_n$ is central with $\omega_\C^{2} = 1$, $P_\pm = \tfrac12(1 \pm \omega_\C)$ split $\Cl = \Cl P_+ \oplus \Cl P_-$ as algebras, $a \mapsto a P_\pm$ maps $\Cl^{+}$ isomorphically onto $\Cl P_\pm$, and $e_j \mapsto \mathrm{i}\, e_j e_n$ ($j < n$) gives $\Cl_{2k}(\C) \cong \Cl^{+}$. These are the types stated in part (i) of \claimref{std-algebra.complexification}, now with $\Cl$ over $\C$ defined directly. Hence
\begin{equation}
\Cl_{2k}(\C) \cong \Mat_{2^{k}}(\C), \qquad \Cl_{2k+1}(\C) \cong \Mat_{2^{k}}(\C) \oplus \Mat_{2^{k}}(\C), \qquad \Cl_{n+2}(\C) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl_n(\C)).
\end{equation}
\item[(v)] In the Fock model of (iii), with $\tau$ the reversal of $\Lambda W$ and $\operatorname{top}$ the coefficient of $w_1 \wedge \dots \wedge w_k$, the $\C$-bilinear form $\beta(x, y) = \operatorname{top}(\tau x \wedge y)$ is nondegenerate, pairs $\Lambda^{m} W$ only with $\Lambda^{k-m} W$, satisfies $\beta(y, x) = (-1)^{k(k-1)/2} \beta(x, y)$ and $\beta(a x, y) = \beta(x, \tilde a y)$ for every $a \in \Cl(V, Q)$. So every complex bivector $A$ is skew, $\beta(Ax, y) + \beta(x, Ay) = 0$, and every $R$ with $\tilde R R = 1$ preserves $\beta$. For $k = 3$ ($\Cl_6(\C) \cong \Cl(2,4) \otimes \C$) the pairing is antisymmetric and pairs $\Lambda^{\mathrm{even}} W$ perfectly with $\Lambda^{\mathrm{odd}} W$.
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.complexification.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] For nondegenerate $V$ of dimension $n$, $\Cl(V, Q) \otimes_\R \C \cong \Cl_n(\C)$, the Clifford algebra of $\C^{n}$ with $Q(z) = \sum z_i^{2}$; it depends only on $n$. $\Cl_n(\C) \cong \Mat_{2^{n/2}}(\C)$ for $n$ even and $\Cl_n(\C) \cong \Mat_{2^{(n-1)/2}}(\C) \oplus \Mat_{2^{(n-1)/2}}(\C)$ for $n$ odd.
\item[(ii)] Let $n$ be odd, $V$ nondegenerate, and $\omega = e_1 \cdots e_n$ for an orthonormal basis ($Q(e_i) = \pm 1$), so that $\omega^{2} = \pm 1$. Then $\omega$ is central and invertible, $\Cl^{-} = \Cl^{+}\omega$, and
\begin{itemize}
\item if $\omega^{2} = 1$, the central idempotents $P_{\pm} = \tfrac12(1 \pm \omega)$ split $\Cl(V) = \Cl(V) P_+ \oplus \Cl(V) P_-$, and $a \mapsto a P_{\pm}$ is an isomorphism $\Cl^{+} \to \Cl(V) P_{\pm}$, so $\Cl(V) \cong \Cl^{+} \oplus \Cl^{+}$;
\item if $\omega^{2} = -1$, $a \otimes (x + \mathrm{i} y) \mapsto a(x + y\omega)$ is an isomorphism $\Cl^{+} \otimes_\R \C \to \Cl(V)$, with $\omega$ as the imaginary unit.
\end{itemize}
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.contraction-expansion.formal:
For $a \in V$ and $X, Y \in \Cl(V, Q)$:
\begin{align}
a \rfloor (X Y) &= (a \rfloor X) Y + \hat{X} (a \rfloor Y), \\
a \rfloor (X \wedge Y) &= (a \rfloor X) \wedge Y + \hat{X} \wedge (a \rfloor Y), \\
a \rfloor (a \rfloor X) &= 0 .
\end{align}
Under the isomorphism $\Phi$ of \claimref{std-algebra.exterior-isomorphism}, $a \rfloor$ is the interior product of $\Lambda V$ with the linear form $B(a, \cdot)$. Consequently
\begin{equation}
a \rfloor (b_1 \wedge \dots \wedge b_k) = \sum_{i=1}^{k} (-1)^{i-1} B(a, b_i)\, b_1 \wedge \dots \wedge \check{b}_i \wedge \dots \wedge b_k,
\end{equation}
where $\check{b}_i$ means $b_i$ is omitted.

std-algebra.determinant-pseudoscalar.formal:
Let $f : V \to V$ be linear and $I$ any nonzero element of $\Cl^{n}(V, Q)$. Then
\begin{equation}
\underline{f}(I) = \det(f)\, I ,
\end{equation}
so $\det(f g) = \det(f) \det(g)$ and $\underline{f g} = \underline{f}\,\underline{g}$. If $f$ is an isometry, $\underline{f} = \Cl(f)$, the algebra homomorphism of \claimref{std-algebra.functoriality}; in particular an isometry multiplies the pseudoscalar by $\det f = \pm 1$ when $V$ is nondegenerate.

std-algebra.duality.formal:
Let $I$ be the unit pseudoscalar of $\Cl(p, q, r)$. For every signature, right multiplication by $I$ maps $\Cl^{k}$ into $\Cl^{n-k}$, and for $a \in V$
\begin{equation}
a \rfloor (X I) = (a \wedge X) I, \qquad a \wedge (X I) = (a \rfloor X) I .
\end{equation}
If $r = 0$, $I$ is invertible and $X \mapsto X I$ is a linear isomorphism $\Cl^{k} \to \Cl^{n-k}$ (the \emph{duality}); for a nonzero $k$-blade $A$, $A I$ is a nonzero $(n-k)$-blade with $[A I] = [A]^{\perp}$. So outer and inner products are exchanged by duality.

std-algebra.even-subalgebra-isomorphism.formal:
Let $e \in V$ with $Q(e) = s \ne 0$ and $U = e^{\perp}$, so $V = \R e \perp U$ (the form on $U$ may be degenerate). Then $u \mapsto u e$ extends to an isomorphism
\begin{equation}
\Cl(U, -s\,Q|_U) \;\xrightarrow{\ \cong\ }\; \Cl^{+}(V, Q).
\end{equation}
In particular $\Cl^{+}(p, q, r) \cong \Cl(p, q{-}1, r)$ for $q \ge 1$ (take $Q(e) = -1$) and $\Cl^{+}(p, q, r) \cong \Cl(q, p{-}1, r)$ for $p \ge 1$ (take $Q(e) = 1$).

std-algebra.exterior-isomorphism.formal:
The outer product makes $\Cl(V, Q)$ an associative unital graded algebra, and there is a unique algebra isomorphism
\begin{equation}
\Phi : (\Lambda V, \wedge) \longrightarrow (\Cl(V, Q), \wedge), \qquad \Phi(v) = v,
\end{equation}
which maps $\Lambda^{k} V$ onto $\Cl^{k}$. For vectors, $v_1 \wedge \dots \wedge v_k = [v_1, \dots, v_k]$, the antisymmetrised product, and $v \wedge v = 0$. $\Phi^{-1}$ is the symbol map $x \mapsto x \cdot 1$ of the exterior model of \claimref{std-algebra.existence-uniqueness}. In an orthogonal basis, $e_I \wedge e_J$ is $e_I e_J$ when $I \cap J = \emptyset$ and $0$ otherwise. The outer product does not depend on $Q$: as a graded algebra, $(\Cl(V, Q), \wedge)$ is the same for every quadratic form on $V$, degenerate ones included.

std-algebra.fock-representation.formal:
Let $V = W' \oplus W$ with Witt basis $v_i \in W'$, $w_i \in W$ ($1 \le i \le k$) as in \claimref{std-algebra.witt-basis-car}, so $V \cong \R^{k,k}$. On $\Lambda W$ let $w \in W$ act by $w \wedge{}$ and $v \in W'$ by the contraction $\iota_{2B(v, \cdot)}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] this extends to an algebra isomorphism $\Cl(V, Q) \cong \End(\Lambda W) \cong \Mat_{2^{k}}(\R)$;
\item[(ii)] $f = v_1 w_1 v_2 w_2 \cdots v_k w_k$ is a primitive idempotent with $v_i f = 0$ for all $i$; the left ideal $\Cl(V, Q)\, f$ is a spinor space of dimension $2^{k}$ with basis $\{w_I f\}$, and $w_I f \mapsto w_{i_1} \wedge \dots \wedge w_{i_m}$ is an isomorphism of $\Cl(V, Q)$-modules $\Cl(V, Q)\, f \cong \Lambda W$.
\end{enumerate}
So the Fock space is the minimal left ideal generated by the vacuum idempotent $f$, and the $w_I f$ are its occupation-number basis.

std-algebra.grade-decomposition.formal:
Let $e_1, \dots, e_n$ be an orthogonal basis of $V$. Then $\Cl^{k}(V, Q)$ is the span of the blades $e_I$ with $|I| = k$, so $\dim \Cl^{k} = \binom{n}{k}$ and
\begin{equation}
\Cl(V, Q) = \bigoplus_{k=0}^{n} \Cl^{k}(V, Q),
\end{equation}
with $\Cl^{0} = \R\,1$ and $\Cl^{1} = V$. The decomposition does not depend on the orthogonal basis. For $u, v \in V$, $\langle uv \rangle_0 = B(u, v)$ and $\langle uv \rangle_2 = \tfrac12(uv - vu)$.

std-algebra.inverse-of-vector.formal:
In $\Cl(V, Q)$, a vector $v$ is invertible if and only if $Q(v) \ne 0$, and then $v^{-1} = v / Q(v)$. A nonzero null vector satisfies $v^{2} = 0$: it is a zero divisor. A \emph{versor} is a product $g = u_1 u_2 \cdots u_k$ of invertible vectors; it satisfies
\begin{equation}
g \tilde{g} = \tilde{g} g = Q(u_1) Q(u_2) \cdots Q(u_k) \ne 0,
\end{equation}
so $g$ is invertible with $g^{-1} = \tilde{g} / \bigl(Q(u_1) \cdots Q(u_k)\bigr)$. None of this needs the form to be nondegenerate.

std-algebra.involution-properties.formal:
The grade involution is an automorphism, reversion and Clifford conjugation are anti-automorphisms; all three are involutions and they commute. On $\Cl^{k}$ they act by
\begin{equation}
\hat{x} = (-1)^{k} x, \qquad \tilde{x} = (-1)^{k(k-1)/2} x, \qquad \bar{x} = (-1)^{k(k+1)/2} x .
\end{equation}
\texttt{GA.grade\_inv} and \texttt{GA.reverse} implement the first two.

std-algebra.matrix-units.formal:
Let $A$ be an associative unital real algebra containing elements $E_{ij}$, $1 \le i, j \le m$, with
\begin{equation}
E_{ij} E_{kl} = \delta_{jk} E_{il}, \qquad \textstyle\sum_{i} E_{ii} = 1 .
\end{equation}
Then $C = E_{11} A E_{11}$ is a unital algebra (with unit $E_{11}$) and
\begin{equation}
\Phi : A \to \Mat_m(C), \qquad \Phi(b)_{ij} = E_{1i}\, b\, E_{j1},
\end{equation}
is an isomorphism of unital algebras with inverse $(c_{ij}) \mapsto \sum_{i,j} E_{i1} c_{ij} E_{1j}$. In $\Cl(1,1)$, with $f = e_1 e_2$ ($f^{2} = 1$) and $\varepsilon_{\pm} = \tfrac12(1 \pm f)$, the elements $E_{11} = \varepsilon_+$, $E_{12} = \varepsilon_+ e_1$, $E_{21} = \varepsilon_- e_1$, $E_{22} = \varepsilon_-$ are such a system with $C = \R$, so $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$; in $\Cl(1,1,1)$ the same system has a two-dimensional corner $C \cong \Cl(0,0,1)$, so $\Cl(1,1,1) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl(0,0,1))$.

std-algebra.morita-amplification.formal:
Let $A$ be a unital real algebra and $m \ge 1$, and let $E_{ij}$ be the matrix units of $\Mat_m(A)$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] the centre of $\Mat_m(A)$ is $\{z \cdot 1_m : z \in \Zc(A)\}$;
\item[(ii)] the two-sided ideals of $\Mat_m(A)$ are exactly the $\Mat_m(I)$ with $I$ a two-sided ideal of $A$;
\item[(iii)] $M \mapsto M^{m}$ (columns, acted on by matrix multiplication) and $N \mapsto E_{11} N$ are mutually inverse equivalences between left $A$-modules and left $\Mat_m(A)$-modules, so $A$ and $\Mat_m(A)$ are Morita equivalent.
\end{enumerate}
With \claimref{std-algebra.matrix-units}: an algebra with a full $m \times m$ system of matrix units has the centre, the ideals and the modules of its corner.

std-algebra.periodicity.formal:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $\R \otimes \HH = \HH$, $\C \otimes \C \cong \C \oplus \C$, $\C \otimes \HH \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ and $\HH \otimes \HH \cong \Mat_4(\R)$ (all tensor products over $\R$).
\item[(ii)] For $k = 0, \dots, 8$:
\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|ccccccccc}
$k$ & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 & 5 & 6 & 7 & 8 \\ \hline
$\Cl(k,0)$ & $\R$ & $\R^{2}$ & $\Mat_2(\R)$ & $\Mat_2(\C)$ & $\Mat_2(\HH)$ & $\Mat_2(\HH)^{2}$ & $\Mat_4(\HH)$ & $\Mat_8(\C)$ & $\Mat_{16}(\R)$ \\
$\Cl(0,k)$ & $\R$ & $\C$ & $\HH$ & $\HH^{2}$ & $\Mat_2(\HH)$ & $\Mat_4(\C)$ & $\Mat_8(\R)$ & $\Mat_8(\R)^{2}$ & $\Mat_{16}(\R)$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}
where $A^{2} = A \oplus A$.
\item[(iii)] $\Cl(p{+}1, q{+}1) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl(p,q))$ and $\Cl(p{+}8, q) \cong \Cl(p, q{+}8) \cong \Mat_{16}(\Cl(p,q))$. Hence $\Cl(p,q)$ is determined by $n = p + q$ and $p - q \bmod 8$, and is the algebra given in \claimref{primer.classification-real-clifford-algebras}, which this result proves.
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.product-decomposition.formal:
For a vector $a \in V$ and any $X \in \Cl(V, Q)$,
\begin{equation}
a X = a \rfloor X + a \wedge X, \qquad a \rfloor X = \tfrac12\bigl(a X - \hat{X} a\bigr), \qquad a \wedge X = \tfrac12\bigl(a X + \hat{X} a\bigr).
\end{equation}
For $X = X_k \in \Cl^{k}$, $a \rfloor X_k \in \Cl^{k-1}$ and $a \wedge X_k \in \Cl^{k+1}$, so $a X_k$ has only these two grades. In particular $a b = B(a, b) + a \wedge b$ for vectors.

std-algebra.pseudoscalar-commutation.formal:
In $\Cl(p, q, r)$, $n = p + q + r$, with unit pseudoscalar $I$:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $I X_k = (-1)^{k(n-1)} X_k I$ for $X_k \in \Cl^{k}$; so $I$ commutes with $\Cl^{+}$, and with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is odd, while it anticommutes with $\Cl^{-}$ when $n$ is even. Equivalently $I X = X I$ for $n$ odd and $I X = \hat{X} I$ for $n$ even.
\item[(ii)] $I \tilde{I} = \prod_{i} Q(e_i)$, which is $(-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ and $0$ when $r > 0$.
\item[(iii)] $I^{2} = (-1)^{n(n-1)/2} (-1)^{q}$ when $r = 0$ (\claimref{primer.sign}), and $I^{2} = 0$ when $r > 0$: in a degenerate algebra the pseudoscalar is nilpotent.
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.pure-spinors.formal:
In the Fock model of \claimref{std-algebra.fock-representation}, for a nonzero spinor $\psi \in \Lambda W$ let $N_\psi = \{x \in V : x \psi = 0\}$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $N_\psi$ is totally null, so $\dim N_\psi \le k$; $\psi$ is \emph{pure} when $\dim N_\psi = k$;
\item[(ii)] every maximal totally null subspace of $V$ is $N_\psi$ for a pure $\psi$, unique up to a scalar;
\item[(iii)] (Cartan) for $k \le 3$ every nonzero spinor of definite parity is pure; for $k = 4$, $1 + w_1 w_2 w_3 w_4$ is not;
\item[(iv)] (Thouless form) for every antisymmetric $k \times k$ matrix $Z$, $\psi_Z = \exp\bigl(\sum_{i<j} Z_{ij}\, w_i \wedge w_j\bigr)$ is pure, with $N_{\psi_Z}$ spanned by $v_i - \sum_j Z_{ij} w_j$.
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.radical-ideal-and-quotient.formal:
Let $(V, Q)$ be a real quadratic space of dimension $n$ whose radical $\operatorname{rad} V$ (\claimref{obj.std-algebra.radical}) has dimension $r$, let $J = \Cl(V, Q)\, \operatorname{rad} V\, \Cl(V, Q)$ be the two-sided ideal it generates, and let $\bar V = V / \operatorname{rad} V$ with $\bar Q(v + \operatorname{rad} V) = Q(v)$. Let $U$ be any complement of $\operatorname{rad} V$, with basis $e_1, \dots, e_m$ ($m = n - r$), and $f_1, \dots, f_r$ a basis of $\operatorname{rad} V$. Then:
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] $f a = \hat{a} f$ for every $f \in \operatorname{rad} V$ and $a \in \Cl(V, Q)$; hence $J = \Cl(V, Q)\operatorname{rad} V = \operatorname{rad} V\, \Cl(V, Q)$, and $J$ is spanned by the blades $e_I f_K$ with $K \ne \emptyset$, so $\dim J = 2^{n} - 2^{n-r}$.
\item[(ii)] For $k \ge 1$, $J^{k}$ is spanned by the blades $e_I f_K$ with $\lvert K \rvert \ge k$. So $J^{r+1} = 0$, and $J^{r} = \Cl(U) f_1 \cdots f_r \ne 0$ when $r \ge 1$: the nilpotency index of $J$ is exactly $r + 1$.
\item[(iii)] $\bar Q$ is well defined and nondegenerate, the map $V \to \bar V$ extends to a surjective algebra homomorphism $\pi : \Cl(V, Q) \to \Cl(\bar V, \bar Q)$ with kernel exactly $J$, and $\pi$ restricts to an isomorphism $\Cl(U, Q|_U) \to \Cl(\bar V, \bar Q)$. So
\begin{equation}
\Cl(V, Q) / J \cong \Cl(\bar V, \bar Q), \qquad \Cl(V, Q) = \Cl(U, Q|_U) \oplus J ,
\end{equation}
the second a direct sum of a subalgebra and an ideal.
\item[(iv)] $J$ is the largest nilpotent left (or right, or two-sided) ideal of $\Cl(V, Q)$, and it is the radical of the bilinear form $(x, y) \mapsto \langle x y \rangle_0$. In particular $J$ does not depend on choices, and the nondegenerate algebra $\Cl(\bar V, \bar Q)$ has no nonzero nilpotent one-sided ideal.
\end{enumerate}
For $r = 1$: $J^{2} = 0$ and $\dim J = 2^{n-1}$. Example: for a null vector $k$ of $\R^{1,3}$ the null hyperplane $k^{\perp}$ has radical $\R k$; its Clifford algebra is the subalgebra of $\Cl(1,3)$ generated by $k^{\perp}$, $J = k\,\Cl(k^{\perp})$ squares to zero, the generators $N = k a$ ($a \in k^{\perp}$) of the null rotations $1 + N$ about $k$ are the bivectors of $J$, so they commute, multiply to zero and have $e^{N} = 1 + N$, and $\Cl(k^{\perp})/J \cong \Cl(0,2)$.

std-algebra.reflection.formal:
Let $u \in V$ with $Q(u) \ne 0$. Then $V = \R u \oplus u^{\perp}$, and for $x \in V$
\begin{equation}
-\,u x u^{-1} = x - 2\,\frac{B(x, u)}{Q(u)}\, u .
\end{equation}
The map $s_u : x \mapsto -u x u^{-1}$ is an isometry of $(V, Q)$ fixing $u^{\perp}$ pointwise and sending $u$ to $-u$: the reflection in the hyperplane $u^{\perp}$. It fixes $\operatorname{rad} V$ pointwise, $s_u^{2} = 1$, $s_{\lambda u} = s_u$ for $\lambda \ne 0$, and its determinant is $-1$. If $Q(a) = Q(b) \ne 0$ and $Q(a - b) \ne 0$, then $s_{a-b}(a) = b$.

std-algebra.rotors-beyond-exponentials.formal:
Let $(V, Q)$ be a nondegenerate real quadratic space of signature $(p, q)$, $n = p + q$, and write $\Ad_R(x) = R x R^{-1}$ for even $R$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] For every bivector $B$, every negative real eigenvalue of $\Ad_{e^{B}}$ on $V$, and so of $\Ad_{-e^{B}} = \Ad_{e^{B}}$, has even algebraic multiplicity. (Here $\Ad_{e^{B}} = \exp(X)$ with $X = \operatorname{ad}_{B}|_{V}$ a real linear map of $V$, the series identity of \claimref{std-algebra.bivector-exponential-in-spin}(ii).)
\item[(ii)] (A sufficient condition.) If $p \ge 2$ and $q \ge 2$, let $e_1, e_2, e_3, e_4$ be orthonormal with $Q(e_1) = Q(e_2) = 1$ and $Q(e_3) = Q(e_4) = -1$, and
\begin{equation}
R \;=\; -\,e_1 e_2 e_3 e_4 \bigl(\tfrac54 + \tfrac34\, e_1 e_3\bigr) .
\end{equation}
Then $R$ is a rotor ($R$ is a product of six unit vectors and $R\tilde R = 1$), and $\Ad_R$ has the eigenvalues $-4$ and $-\tfrac14$ on $\operatorname{span}(e_1, e_3)$, $-1$ on $e_2$ and $e_4$, and $1$ on $\{e_1, \dots, e_4\}^{\perp}$. Since $-4$ is a simple eigenvalue, neither $R$ nor $-R$ is the exponential of any bivector. So for every signature with $\min(p, q) \ge 2$, among them the conformal algebra $\Cl(2,4)$ and the split algebras $\Cl(k,k)$ with $k \ge 2$, the rotors are not all of the form $\pm e^{B}$.
\item[(iii)] (Low dimensions.) For $n = 1$ there are no nonzero bivectors and $-1$ is a rotor; in $\Cl(1,1)$ the rotors are $a + b\,e_1e_2$ with $a^{2} - b^{2} = 1$, every exponential $e^{\lambda e_1 e_2} = \cosh\lambda + \sinh\lambda\, e_1 e_2$ has $a \ge 1$, and $-1$ is a rotor. In both cases $-1$ is a rotor that is not an exponential, although every rotor is $\pm e^{B}$.
\end{enumerate}

std-algebra.spin-double-cover.formal:
Let $(V, Q)$ be nondegenerate of dimension $n \ge 1$. The twisted adjoint action gives surjective homomorphisms with kernel $\{\pm 1\}$:
\begin{equation}
\Pin(V, Q) \to \mathrm{O}(V, Q), \qquad \Spin(V, Q) \to \mathrm{SO}(V, Q), \qquad \Spin^{+}(V, Q) \to \mathrm{SO}^{+}(V, Q).
\end{equation}
Here $\mathrm{SO}^{+}$ is the kernel of the spinor norm, as defined in \claimref{obj.std-algebra.pin-spin}; for nondegenerate $V$ it is the identity component of $\mathrm{SO}(V, Q)$, a standard fact cited there and not proved in the store. A versor of $k$ factors acts with determinant $(-1)^{k}$. For a rotor, $\Ad_R(x) = R x \tilde{R}$. Nondegeneracy is needed for the kernel: for degenerate forms $\Ad$ has a larger kernel or a smaller image.

std-algebra.square-roots-of-minus-one.formal:
Let $A$ be a real algebra $\Mat_m(D)$, $D \in \{\R, \C, \HH\}$. The elements $f \in A$ with $f^{2} = -1$, up to conjugation $f \mapsto g f g^{-1}$ by invertible $g \in A$, are:
\begin{itemize}
\item $D = \R$: none if $m$ is odd; one class if $m$ is even;
\item $D = \C$: $m + 1$ classes, $f$ being conjugate to $\operatorname{diag}(\mathrm{i} 1_k, -\mathrm{i} 1_{m-k})$ for exactly one $k \in \{0, \dots, m\}$;
\item $D = \HH$: one class.
\end{itemize}
In $A \oplus A$ the classes are the pairs of classes. Consequently (\claimref{std-algebra.periodicity}) a nondegenerate $\Cl(p,q)$ contains a square root of $-1$ unless $(p, q) \in \{(0,0), (1,0)\}$; in $\Cl(1,3) \cong \Mat_2(\HH)$ every square root of $-1$, for example a unit spacelike vector or the pseudoscalar $I$, is conjugate to $I$; and in $\Cl(3,0) \cong \Mat_2(\C)$ there are three classes, those of $\omega$, $-\omega$ and $e_1e_2$, separated by $\langle f \omega^{-1} \rangle_0 \in \{1, -1, 0\}$.

std-algebra.tensor-rules.formal:
Let $(V, Q)$ be any real quadratic space (degenerate allowed) and $(W, Q_W)$ a nondegenerate one of even dimension, with pseudoscalar $\omega_W$ and $\omega_W^{2} = \epsilon \in \{1, -1\}$. Then
\begin{equation}
\Cl(V \perp W,\ \epsilon Q \oplus Q_W) \;\cong\; \Cl(V, Q) \otimes_{\R} \Cl(W, Q_W), \qquad v \mapsto v \otimes \omega_W, \quad w \mapsto 1 \otimes w .
\end{equation}
For the planes and for $\R^{0,8}$, $\R^{8,0}$ this gives, with $\Cl(1,1) \cong \Cl(2,0) \cong \Mat_2(\R)$:
\begin{equation}
\Cl(p{+}1, q{+}1) \cong \Mat_2(\Cl(p,q)), \quad \Cl(q{+}2, p) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(2,0), \quad \Cl(q, p{+}2) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(0,2),
\end{equation}
and $\Cl(p, q{+}8) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(0,8)$, $\Cl(p{+}8, q) \cong \Cl(p,q) \otimes \Cl(8,0)$; all of these hold with a radical $\R^{0,0,r}$ added to $(p, q)$.

std-algebra.vector-squares.formal:
In $\Cl(V, Q)$, for all $u, v \in V$,
\begin{equation}
v^{2} = Q(v), \qquad u v + v u = 2 B(u, v).
\end{equation}
In particular $u v = B(u, v) + \tfrac12(u v - v u)$, the sum of a scalar and an antisymmetric part.

std-algebra.witt-basis-car.formal:
Let $v_1, \dots, v_k, w_1, \dots, w_k \in V$ be a \emph{Witt basis} of their span: $B(v_i, v_j) = B(w_i, w_j) = 0$ and $B(v_i, w_j) = \tfrac12 \delta_{ij}$. Then in $\Cl(V, Q)$
\begin{equation}
v_i v_j + v_j v_i = 0, \qquad w_i w_j + w_j w_i = 0, \qquad v_i w_j + w_j v_i = \delta_{ij},
\end{equation}
the canonical anticommutation relations for $k$ modes (read $v_i$ as annihilators, $w_i$ as creators). The elements $N_i = w_i v_i$ are mutually commuting idempotents with $N_i + v_i w_i = 1$. In particular, for orthogonal hyperbolic planes $\operatorname{span}(e_i, e_{k+i})$ of $\R^{k,k}$, $v_i = \tfrac12(e_i + e_{k+i})$ and $w_i = \tfrac12(e_i - e_{k+i})$ form such a basis. The form need not be nondegenerate outside the span.

std-algebra.witt-decomposition.formal:
Let $(V, Q)$ be a real quadratic space of signature $(p, q, r)$ and let $m = \min(p, q)$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] If $P \subseteq V$ is a nondegenerate plane and $u \in P$ is a nonzero null vector, there is a unique $w \in P$ such that $(u, w)$ is a hyperbolic pair; $P$ is a hyperbolic plane.
\item[(ii)] (Witt decomposition) There is an orthogonal decomposition
\begin{equation}
V = H_1 \perp \dots \perp H_m \perp A \perp \operatorname{rad} V
\end{equation}
with each $H_i$ a hyperbolic plane and $A$ anisotropic ($Q(a) \ne 0$ for $a \ne 0$, hence definite, of signature $(p - m, q - m, 0)$).
\item[(iii)] If $(u_i, w_i)$ is a hyperbolic pair spanning $H_i$, then $N = \operatorname{span}(u_1, \dots, u_m) \oplus \operatorname{rad} V$ is a maximal totally null subspace, and every totally null subspace has dimension at most $m + r$.
\end{enumerate}
